# {{PROJECT_NAME}} research instructions

Use the project's versioned `RESEARCH_WORKFLOW.md` for locations, ownership,
evidence requirements, and acceptance policy. Updating a global skill does not
automatically change the adopted project workflow.

## Select the task and its inputs

- For research, identify the assigned study and read its current README plus
  the relevant maintained sources. A materially different question normally
  starts a new study; routine corrections and validation continue the same one.
- For a bounded worker or independent reviewer, follow the explicit assignment
  and its source boundary. Report missing inputs instead of retrieving more
  provisional research. Required operating instructions remain available.
- For a read-only question, inspect only its relevant permitted sources.
- For maintenance, use assigned paths and preserve concurrent work.
- For promotion, read the complete local acceptance policy and its required
  evidence before declaring a candidate ready for integration.

Studies organize investigations; tasks organize conversations and work. Do not
read another study's provisional findings merely because they appear relevant.
Use only explicitly authorized cross-study inputs, preserving their versions,
provisional status, and any resulting limits on independence.

## Preserve scientific and operational boundaries

Keep scientific validity, checking status, and adoption status distinct. A
self-check is not an independent review. Accepted theory and code must contain
their actual arguments, implementation, and reproduction dependencies.

Keep one useful current record per study; link to evidence rather than copying
it into several administrative documents. Retain negative results and label
superseded conclusions. Trace generated results and figures to actual inputs,
source, configurations, and runs under the local storage policy.

Use familiar domain notation and agreed conventions, define central objects,
and make mathematical passages self-contained for their requested scope. Use
specialized research, proof, teaching, or review skills when applicable; they
do not replace this project's source boundaries or acceptance requirements.

Follow the local ownership and Git coordination policy. Do not overwrite or
stage another contributor's changes. A workflow, old plan, or directory layout
does not by itself authorize new experiments, wider access, or integration of
an unchecked scientific claim.

Read the relevant adopted rules again when they change. Respect authorization
already given for a concrete action; do not request it again for the same scope.
